[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Containers, Stacks, and GeometryReader Anti-Patterns in SwiftUI"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"SwiftUI layout is built on a declarative, pass-by-pass negotiation model where parents propose sizes to children, children choose their own size, and parents then position the children. Misunderstanding this layout engine frequently leads to anti-patterns, particularly when misusing containers like VStack, HStack, ZStack, and the layout-breaking GeometryReader."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Anti-Pattern 1: Greedy GeometryReader at the Root"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"One of the most common mistakes is wrapping a view hierarchy inside a GeometryReader simply to get screen dimensions or to make a view expand. GeometryReader is a layout container that unconditionally accepts all space offered by its parent and then frames its child to that exact size."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// ANTI-PATTERN
GeometryReader { proxy in
    VStack {
        Text("Hello World")
    }
    .frame(width: proxy.size.width * 0.8)
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the code above, the GeometryReader disrupts the natural layout pass. It forces its coordinate space and sizing onto the parent, which often results in misaligned views and unexpected clipping. Instead of using GeometryReader for sizing, prefer native modifiers like .frame(maxWidth: .infinity) or layout priorities."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Anti-Pattern 2: Deeply Nested Stacks for Alignment"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Developers transitioning from UIKit often recreate complex Auto Layout hierarchies by nesting multiple HStacks, VStacks, and ZStacks deep within each other. This creates severe performance bottlenecks because SwiftUI evaluates layout passes for every node in the tree."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// ANTI-PATTERN
VStack {
    HStack {
        VStack {
            Text("Title")
            HStack {
                Text("Subtitle")
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Deeply nested stacks increase compile times for the Swift type checker exponentially."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Runtime layout calculations slow down as the view tree expands in depth rather than breadth."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Solution: Use layout priority, custom layouts (Layout protocol), or simplify the design to reduce structural complexity."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Anti-Pattern 3: Hardcoded Magic Numbers"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Relying on hardcoded sizes like .frame(width: 400, height: 800) breaks responsiveness across different device form factors. SwiftUI is designed to be fluid and adapt to content size categories and screen dimensions automatically."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// CORRECT APPROACH
VStack {
    Text("Adaptive Content")
        .font(.headline)
}
.frame(maxWidth: .infinity, maxHeight: .infinity)
.background(Color.blue)"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Clean SwiftUI Layouts"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Let content drive sizing: Allow views to size themselves based on their content before overriding with explicit frames."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Isolate GeometryReader: If you must use GeometryReader for reading size or positioning animations, place it deep inside the leaf views rather than at the root of a screen layout."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage ViewThatFits: Use ViewThatFits to automatically adapt layouts to horizontal or vertical constraints without manual conditional branching."}]
